It guarantees that the equation has an equilibrium $x^*$.","If $a_0(t)=0$, then $x_t$ would not occur and the order would be below $k$."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"5d3099277987562bfc4e4ec5","time_limit":40,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-27T08:58:47.943104+00:00","reason_code":"replaced_after_academic_review"},{"id":"year_2_1a_math_v_advanced_linear_algebra_difference_equation_718cae88_014","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"A second-order linear difference equation reads $x_{t+2}-x_{t+1}+0.5\\,x_t=3$ for $t=0,1,2,\\dots$. Which constant sequence $x_t=x^*$ solves it?","options":["$x^*=3$","$x^*=2$","$x^*=6$","$x^*=1.2$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"85e00cc0d79a211aab89ab6b","time_limit":30,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-27T08:58:47.943104+00:00","reason_code":"duplicate_diagnostic_target"},{"id":"year_2_1a_math_v_advanced_linear_algebra_difference_equation_718cae88_015","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"Which statement about the equilibria of the difference equation $x_{t+1}=x_t+2$, $t=0,1,2,\\dots$, is correct?","options":["$x^*=0$, as for every linear difference equation.","$x^*=-2$, obtained by setting $x_t+2=0$.","Every constant is one, since $x_{t+1}-x_t$ is constant.","There is none, since $x^*=x^*+2$ has no solution."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"926b1e6e126d2b95eafb096b","time_limit":30,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-27T08:58:47.943104+00:00","reason_code":"replaced_after_academic_review"},{"id":"year_2_1a_math_v_advanced_linear_algebra_difference_equation_718cae88_019","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"application","question":"Let $\\Delta x_t=x_{t+1}-x_t$ and $\\Delta^2x_t=\\Delta x_{t+1}-\\Delta x_t$. Written with shifted terms, what is the equation $\\Delta^2x_t+\\Delta x_t=0$, $t=0,1,2,\\dots$, and its order?","options":["$x_{t+2}=x_{t+1}$, which has order $2$","$x_{t+2}-3x_{t+1}+2x_t=0$, of order $2$","$x_{t+2}+x_{t+1}-2x_t=0$, of order $2$","$x_{t+2}=x_{t+1}$, which has order $1$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"4d828a5b62a4cc109393d7bf","time_limit":40,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-27T08:58:47.943104+00:00","reason_code":"replaced_after_academic_review"},{"id":"year_2_1a_math_v_advanced_linear_algebra_difference_equation_718cae88_020","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"application","question":"Two sequences $u$ and $v$ both solve $x_{t+2}-x_{t+1}-x_t=1$, $t=0,1,2,\\dots$. Which of the following sequences is guaranteed to solve the same equation?","options":["$2u-v$","$u+v$","$u-v$","$3u$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"17c039990a5ac694d22c7c8f","time_limit":40,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-27T08:58:47.943104+00:00","reason_code":"replaced_after_academic_review"}]}}